B.a9b=s(["In the ashram at Yellapur, the first sound of the day is not an alarm. It is the low hum of a few students chanting in the dark, about an hour and a half before the sun comes up. This time has a name: Brahma muhurta, the creator\u2019s hour.","A muhurta is an old unit of time, about forty-eight minutes long. Brahma muhurta is the one that ends just before sunrise. Traditional texts call it the best time for study, prayer and reflection. The reasons they give are simple, and you can test them yourself.","First, the world is quiet. Traffic, phones and conversations have not started yet. The mind takes its cue from what is around it, and when the surroundings are still, it settles more easily.","Second, the mind itself is fresh. After sleep, yesterday\u2019s worries have not fully returned. Whatever you place in the mind at this hour \u2014 a mantra, a verse, a kind thought \u2014 tends to stay with you through the day.","Third, there is rhythm in it. When you chant at the same hour every day, the body begins to expect it. After a few weeks you may find yourself waking a minute before the alarm, already half-ready to sit.","You do not need to start at four in the morning. Begin fifteen minutes earlier than you wake now, and keep that for a month. Wash your face, sit facing the east if you can, and chant one short mantra slowly. That is enough.","If mornings are impossible because of work or small children, do not feel left out. Chant when you can, with attention. The scriptures praise the dawn, but they praise sincerity even more."],t.s)
B.a4l=s([1,5],t.Y)
B.ahi=new A.lw("I work night shifts. Can the hour after I wake up be my \u201csunrise\u201d?","Yes, in spirit. Choose a fixed, quiet time soon after you have rested and before your duties begin. Regularity matters more than the clock. Keep it short and kind to your body.",6,40)
B.ahm=new A.lw("Should I chant aloud if others at home are still asleep?","A soft voice, a whisper, or silent chanting with gently moving lips are all valid. The tradition lists them in that order \u2014 aloud, whispered, mental \u2014 and each has its place. Never disturb others for your practice.",5,26)
B.a4x=s([B.ahi,B.ahm],t.Fn)
B.a85=s(["Namaste {name}. Thank you for this question. Start where you are: if waking earlier is hard, move your alarm by ten minutes each week rather than all at once. What matters most is sitting at the same time every day and chanting with attention. The quiet hour slowly becomes a habit, not a struggle.","Namaste {name}. A good question. The texts praise Brahma muhurta because the mind is fresh and the surroundings are still \u2014 but they never ask us to harm our health for it. Sleep well, rise a little earlier, and keep the practice short and regular. Regularity is the real secret."],t.s)
B.LG=new A.cd(0.35,-0.4)
B.Mc=new A.kZ("why-sunrise",!1,"Practice","Daily practice","Why we chant at sunrise",4,2,"The hour before dawn \u2014 Brahma muhurta \u2014 is quiet outside and inside. Why the old teachers chose it, and how to begin gently.",B.a9b,B.a4l,"Whatever you place in the mind at this hour tends to stay with you through the day.",3,B.a4x,B.a85,"assets/images/guruji_teaching.jpg",B.LG,1.5,!0,null)
B.a8T=s(["Many people buy a copy of the Bhagavad G\u012bt\u0101, read the first chapter with great enthusiasm, and quietly put it back on the shelf. It is not a lack of devotion. The G\u012bt\u0101 is simply not a book to be finished in a week.","The old way of study was slow on purpose. A student would learn one verse \u2014 first by ear, then by heart \u2014 and only then ask what it meant. A single verse could be carried for days, turned over in the mind like a smooth stone from a river.","You can do the same at home. Choose one verse a week. Read it aloud each morning, slowly, three times. Write it on a card and keep it where you will see it \u2014 on the kitchen shelf, beside your desk, in your pocket.","Then let the verse meet your day. When you are anxious about a result, remember 2.47: your right is to the work, not to its fruits. When you feel small or afraid, remember that Arjuna felt the same before he listened.","Read a good translation beside the Sanskrit, but do not hurry to settle the meaning. Different teachers draw out different layers. Write down your own questions \u2014 they are the beginning of real study, not a sign that you are doing it wrong.","This December, the \u015ar\u012bmad Bhagavad G\u012bt\u0101 Yaj\xf1a at Rushikulam gives twenty-one days to this kind of slow reading, with chanting and study every day. But a single verse on a card, kept faithfully, is already a small yaj\xf1a of its own."],t.s)
B.a4s=s([2,4],t.Y)
B.aho=new A.lw("Which translation of the G\u012bt\u0101 should I read?","Choose one that gives the Sanskrit verse, a word-by-word meaning and a plain translation. Read it beside your teacher\u2019s explanations rather than alone. The course \u201cBhagavad Gita \u2014 first steps\u201d in this section is a gentle place to begin.",4,60)
B.a6X=s([B.aho],t.Fn)
B.a7H=s(["Namaste {name}. Thank you for this question. Go slowly \u2014 one verse lived with for a week teaches more than a chapter read in a hurry. Chant it aloud, keep it on a card, and notice where it meets your day.","Namaste {name}. A good question. The G\u012bt\u0101 opens through regular reading and honest questions. Keep a small notebook for your questions and bring them to class \u2014 or to the G\u012bt\u0101 Yaj\xf1a this December."],t.s)
B.LC=new A.cd(0.1,0.1)
B.M9=new A.kZ("gita-slowly",!1,"Scripture","Scripture","Reading the G\u012bt\u0101 slowly",4,5,"Seven hundred verses can feel like a mountain. A gentle way to read the Bhagavad G\u012bt\u0101 one verse at a time \u2014 and let it read you.",B.a8T,B.a4s,"A single verse could be carried for days, turned over in the mind like a smooth stone from a river.",1,B.a6X,B.a7H,"assets/images/manuscripts.jpg",B.LC,1.7,!1,null)
B.a5D=s(["Almost every chant you learn in this app begins with Om. It is so common that we can stop noticing it. This short piece is an invitation to notice it again.","Om is written as one syllable, but the old teachers hear four parts in it: A, U, M, and the silence that follows. The Mandukya Upanishad, one of the shortest Upanishads, is devoted almost entirely to these four.","\u201cA\u201d is linked with the waking state \u2014 the world we see and touch. \u201cU\u201d is linked with the dream state \u2014 the inner world of images and thoughts. \u201cM\u201d is linked with deep sleep, where both worlds rest. The silence after the sound points to what is present in all three and touched by none of them.","You do not have to believe this to benefit from chanting Om. Try it: take a comfortable breath, open the mouth for \u201cA\u201d, round the lips for \u201cU\u201d, and close them for a long, humming \u201cM\u201d. Then sit in the quiet for a few seconds. Most people feel calmer after just three rounds.","In the tradition, Om is also called pranava \u2014 that which is ever new, or that which carries us across. It is chanted at the start of study and prayer as a way of saying: I am beginning with my whole attention.","Do not rush it, and do not force a loud voice. A gentle, steady Om chanted with care is worth more than a hundred hurried ones."],t.s)
B.a4r=s([2,3],t.Y)
B.ahq=new A.lw("Is it \u201cOm\u201d or \u201cAum\u201d? Which one is correct?","Both describe the same syllable. \u201cAum\u201d shows its three sounds; \u201cOm\u201d is how it sounds when they blend. When chanting, let A and U flow into one rounded O, and give the M time to hum.",1,150)
B.ahl=new A.lw("How many times should I chant Om?","Three is a good start \u2014 it is traditional and easy to keep. If you want more, use the counter in the player and try 11 or 21. Stop before it starts to feel like a task.",3,72)
B.a5H=s([B.ahq,B.ahl],t.Fn)
B.aa1=s(["Namaste {name}. Thank you for asking. Chant Om slowly \u2014 let the \u201cA\u201d and \u201cU\u201d flow into each other and give the \u201cM\u201d a long, gentle hum. Then rest in the silence for a breath or two. That silence is part of the chant, not a gap after it.","Namaste {name}. Many students ask this. You do not need to understand every layer of meaning before you begin. Chant with care, notice how you feel afterwards, and the meaning will open up slowly through practice and study with your teacher."],t.s)
B.Mb=new A.kZ("meaning-of-om",!1,"Sound & meaning","Sound & meaning","The meaning of Om",3,9,"One syllable, three sounds and a silence. A short, plain guide to what Om holds and how to chant it.",B.a5D,B.a4r,"A gentle, steady Om chanted with care is worth more than a hundred hurried ones.",4,B.a5H,B.aa1,null,B.p,1.5,!1,"om-art")
B.a6J=s(["Many people tell us they would like to meditate but have no time. When we ask how long they think it takes, the answer is usually \u201cat least an hour\u201d. It does not. Five minutes done every day will change more than an hour done once a month.","Here is a simple way to sit. Choose the same place each day \u2014 a corner of a room is enough. Sit on the floor or on a chair with your back upright but not stiff, and rest your hands on your knees.","Minute one: just notice your breath. Do not change it. Feel the air coming in, and going out.","Minutes two to four: on each out-breath, silently say one word that means something to you \u2014 Om, Rama, Shanti, or simply \u201cpeace\u201d. When the mind wanders, and it will, gently come back to the word. Coming back is the practice.","Minute five: let the word go and sit quietly. Then open your eyes, and before you get up, think of one thing you are grateful for.","That is all. Do not judge the sitting as good or bad. Some days the mind is a busy market, some days it is a still lake. Both days count.","After a month, if you like, add a minute. But keeping five minutes every day is better than reaching for twenty and then stopping."],t.s)
B.yk=s([3,5],t.Y)
B.ahj=new A.lw("I fall asleep as soon as I close my eyes. What should I do?","Sit a little straighter and keep the eyes half open, resting softly on the floor about a metre ahead. Sitting after a morning wash also helps. Sleepiness is very common at the start and usually passes in a few weeks.",1,300)
B.a6n=s([B.ahj],t.Fn)
B.a9x=s(["Namaste {name}. Thank you for sharing this. A wandering mind is not a sign of failure \u2014 noticing that it wandered and gently coming back is the practice itself. Keep the five minutes, keep the same place and time, and be kind to yourself.","Namaste {name}. Good question. If five minutes feel long, begin with three and add a minute only when it feels easy. A fixed time \u2014 after your morning wash, or before dinner \u2014 helps more than any technique."],t.s)
B.Ma=new A.kZ("five-quiet-minutes",!1,"Practice","Meditation","Five quiet minutes a day",3,16,"You do not need an hour to begin. A simple five-minute sitting you can keep for life.",B.a6J,B.yk,"When the mind wanders, gently come back. Coming back is the practice.",4,B.a6n,B.a9x,null,B.p,1.5,!1,null)
B.a9Z=s(["In many homes in Karnataka, a little of the cooked rice is set aside before anyone eats. It may be placed before a lamp, given to a cow or a crow, or simply offered in the mind. It is one of the oldest habits we have.","The idea behind it is gentle. Food is not something we made from nothing. Sun, rain, soil, farmers, carriers and cooks all had a part in it. Offering it first is a way of saying thank you before we take.","The Bhagavad G\u012bt\u0101 (9.26) speaks of an offering of a leaf, a flower, a fruit or a little water, given with devotion. The point is not the size of the offering but the feeling behind it.","Many students chant a short verse before meals, also from the G\u012bt\u0101 (4.24), which begins \u201cbrahm\u0101rpa\u1e47a\u1e43 brahma havi\u1e25\u201d. It reminds us that the one who offers, the offering and the act of eating all belong to one whole.","There is a practical side too. When we pause for a few seconds before eating, we eat more slowly, notice the taste and stop when we are satisfied. The old habit turns out to be good for digestion as well.","You can start tonight. Before your meal, sit for a moment, look at the food, and silently say thank you \u2014 to whoever or whatever you feel grateful to. That is already an offering."],t.s)
B.ahn=new A.lw("I eat at the office canteen. Can I still make an offering?","Of course. An offering made in the mind is complete. Pause for one breath, look at the plate and give thanks inwardly. No one needs to notice \u2014 the feeling is the same.",5,500)
B.ahk=new A.lw("Where can I learn the full \u201cbrahm\u0101rpa\u1e47am\u201d verse?","Ask your teacher at the next class \u2014 it is one of the first verses students learn, and it is best learnt by ear, line by line. Guruji\u2019s team also plans to add it to the Chant tab.",3,200)
B.a9v=s([B.ahn,B.ahk],t.Fn)
B.a6p=s(["Namaste {name}. Thank you for asking. An offering is complete when it is made with a grateful heart \u2014 the place, the plate or the words come second. Even a silent \u201cthank you\u201d before the first bite is a true offering.","Namaste {name}. A thoughtful question. Simple, fresh food offered with gratitude is the traditional ideal, but the tradition is practical: do what you can in your situation, and let the habit of pausing before meals grow naturally."],t.s)
B.LI=new A.cd(-0.45,-0.15)
B.M8=new A.kZ("food-as-offering",!0,"Daily life","Daily life","Food as an offering",3,24,"Why the tradition asks us to offer food before we eat \u2014 and a simple pause you can bring to your own table.",B.a9Z,B.yh,"The point is not the size of the offering but the feeling behind it.",2,B.a9v,B.a6p,"assets/images/forest.jpg",B.LI,1.3333333333333333,!1,null)
B.a76=s(["The word guru is often explained as \u201cthe one who removes darkness\u201d. It is a beautiful meaning, but it can make the guru sound distant, like someone who switches on a light for us. In practice the gift is more ordinary, and more precious.","A real teacher first gives attention. In a busy world, being truly listened to is rare. Students often say the first thing they felt at the ashram was that someone heard them out without hurry.","A teacher gives a method. Not only ideas, but a way of practising \u2014 how to sit, how to chant, how to study a verse, what to do when the mind is restless. The method is what you carry home.","A teacher gives correction. This is not always pleasant. A good teacher will point out the wrong note in a chant, or the pride hidden in a question. Correction given with care is a form of respect: it means the teacher believes you can grow.","A teacher gives an example. Much of what we learn from a guru is never said at all. We watch how they speak to a visitor, how they meet a difficult day, how they eat and how they rest.","And finally, a teacher gives you back to yourself. The tradition does not want students to depend on the guru for ever. Like a mother teaching a child to walk, the teacher steps back a little more each day \u2014 and is happiest when the student can stand alone.","So if you wonder what to ask of a teacher, ask for practice, honesty and patience. The rest grows by itself."],t.s)
B.ahp=new A.lw("How can I tell whether a teacher is genuine?","Watch over time rather than judging quickly. A genuine teacher is consistent, welcomes honest questions, never asks for blind obedience and is glad when you grow independent. If you ever feel pressured or afraid, step back and speak to people you trust.",5,700)
B.a9t=s([B.ahp],t.Fn)
B.a5o=s(["Namaste {name}. Thank you for this honest question. A teacher\u2019s role is to guide you towards standing on your own feet. Bring your questions to class, practise what you are taught, and notice the small changes in your daily life \u2014 that is how the relationship grows.","Namaste {name}. Good question. Learning from a teacher is less about receiving answers and more about receiving a method and an example. Stay regular with your practice; clarity comes from doing, and your teacher is there to correct the course."],t.s)
B.Lz=new A.cd(-0.15,0.1)
B.Md=new A.kZ("what-a-guru-gives",!0,"Teacher & student","Teacher & student","What a guru really gives",4,33,"Not magic, and not answers to everything. A reflection on the quiet gifts of a real teacher.",B.a76,B.yk,"Correction given with care is a form of respect.",4,B.a9t,B.a5o,"assets/images/gurukula_painting.jpg",B.Lz,1.7777777777777777,!1,null)
